<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#old-javanese">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Javanese</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Javanese, or Kawi, is the literary and administrative language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> recorded from the late 8th century, heavily influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. Hundreds of royal charters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> kingdom and later Javanese and Balinese states were written in it, as was a rich court poetry, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjunawiwaha">Arjunawiwaha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-javanese.jpg" alt="Old Javanese" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raden Wijaya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_of_a_stone_stele_written_in_Kawi_script.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court's patronage of Hindu-Buddhist traditions fostered artistic and literary achievements, including the temples of Panataran and the brick architecture of Trowulan, and <b>Old Javanese</b> works such as the Nagarakretagama of Mpu Prapanca (1365), which describes the realm at its height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa ordered the Mahabharata translated into <b>Old Javanese</b> in 996.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Malay used the Pallava and <b>Kawi</b> scripts, known from inscription stones, and Rencong was also used.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>It was translated and retold across India and beyond: in Tamil from the 9th century, in the Javanese <b>Kawi</b> Mahabharata of the late 10th century, in Kannada by Pampa and Kumaravyasa, in the Telugu Andhra Mahabharatam of the 11th to 14th centuries, in Bengali, Odia, Assamese, Marathi, Punjabi…</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal charters in Old Balinese, later <b>Old Javanese</b>, show irrigation communities, ancestors of the subak system, and temples endowed with land.</blockquote><a href="/state/bedulu-kingdom">Bedulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Airlangga's Pucangan inscription of 1041 tells his story in Sanskrit and <b>Old Javanese</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li></ul></section>
